squareUp -- square up a polynomial system
*****************************************

  * Usage: 
        G = squareUp F
        G = squareUp(F,M)
        G = squareUp(F,n)
        G = squareUp(x0,F)
        G = squareUp(p0,x0,F)
        
  * Inputs:
      * F, an instance of the type *note System: (NAGtypes)System,
      * M, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,,  used to square up the system
        (by default a random matrix is picked)
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,,  the number of polynomials to
        be formed (by default, this equals the number of variables)
      * x0, an instance of the type *note Point: (NAGtypes)AbstractPoint,,
        used to compute the dimension of the tangent space (approximately)
      * p0, an instance of the type *note Point: (NAGtypes)AbstractPoint,,
        parameters specialization (in case of a parametric system)
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * block size
      * Field => ..., default value null
      * Strategy => ..., default value null
      * target rank
      * Verbose => ..., default value false
  * Outputs:
      * G, a *note polynomial system: (NAGtypes)PolySystem,

Description
===========

There are two flavors of this method: both aimed at producing a regular
sequence (either global or local).

The first squares up an overdetermined polynomial system (usually assuming that
the user is interested in the isolated solutions; i.e., the components of
dimension 0) and attaches keys *note SquareUpMatrix: squareUp, and *note
SquaredUpSystem: squareUp, to F.

